<description>
Decode a stamp map: read templates, markers, and palette from a tiled grid, then render stamps.

Input structure:
- Grid divided into K tile columns (each N wide) separated by 1-col dashed separators.
- Top band (rows 0..N-1): K template masks, one per tile column.
- Middle band (rows N+1..2N): marker pixels whose color picks a template.
- Bottom band (rows 2N+2..): K palette swatches giving the output color per template.
- Separator rows at rows N and 2N+1 (dashed pattern of sep color).

Rule:
- Each marker's local position (lr, lc) within its tile column determines which N×N output block to stamp.
- The marker's color identifies which template to use; the palette recolors the template.

Key insight: Marker color encodes template index; local position within tile column encodes stamp block.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="eval" puzzle_id="e12f9a14" lines="138-152">
<description>
Emit diagonal beams from framed 2x2 blocks through frame gaps; beams interact on collision.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Wall color (e.g. green) forms 4x4 frames around 2x2 monochrome blocks.
- Each frame has one or more gap pixels where the wall is missing.

Rule:
- Each gap emits a beam of the inner block's color, traveling diagonally outward (direction set by gap position relative to the block).
- Beams paint every cell they cross until they hit the grid edge or collide.
- 90° collision: both beams turn 45° toward their bisector direction; the intersection cell is left blank (1-pixel gap).
- 45° collision: beams merge into a zigzag (alternating cardinal + diagonal steps) with no gap at the meeting point.

Key insight: Two distinct collision rules — 90° produces a gap and single-pixel diagonal lines; 45° produces no gap and thicker zigzag bands.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="eval" puzzle_id="135a2760" lines="6-19">
<description>
Repair noisy periodic patterns inside frame-delimited grid regions.

Input structure:
- Frame color (most common) forms full rows/cols, dividing the grid into rectangular regions.
- bg (second most common) fills empty space; each region contains a small colored tile repeated periodically with a few corrupted pixels.

Rule:
- For each region, find the bounding box of non-bg content.
- Infer the smallest repeating tile that explains the pattern (majority vote per residue position), requiring it repeat at least 2x per axis.
- Replace the region with the clean tiled version.

Key insight: Tile dimensions are unknown and must be inferred; prefer the smallest tile with genuine repetition over one that memorizes the entire region.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="2d0172a1" lines="209-224">
<description>
Convert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.

Input structure:
- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.
- Foreground 4-connected objects are rough closed frames or small filled blobs.
- Frames may contain other frames/blobs; blobs outside a frame remain outside it.

Rule:
- Detect the containment tree of foreground objects using holes of each frame object.
- Replace every blob with one foreground pixel.
- Replace every frame with a rectangular border padded two cells around its direct children.
- Place siblings by their original row/column overlap and order, with one empty cell between canonical objects.

Key insight: The exact wobbly geometry is irrelevant; only enclosure plus relative sibling positions define the output.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="eval" puzzle_id="67e490f4" lines="8-22">
<description>
Fill holes inside a large template frame using shape-matched outside sprites.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. One large monochrome frame object (template).
- Inside the frame: holes of various shapes (non-frame-color regions).
- Outside the frame: small monochrome sprites scattered on bg.

Rule:
- Crop to the frame's bounding box; fill entirely with frame color.
- For each hole, find an outside sprite whose shape matches (rotation/flip allowed).
- Fill the hole with the matched sprite's color. If multiple sprites match, use the most frequent color.

Key insight: Sprites act as shape-coded color keys — their shape identifies which hole they fill, their color determines the fill color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="cbebaa4b" lines="51-65">
<description>
Assemble scattered jigsaw sprites around a central yellow anchor by pairing red connector pixels.

Input structure:
- bg = black (0). Several colored sprites scattered on the grid.
- One anchor sprite contains a 3×3 yellow (4) block.
- Each sprite has red (2) connector pixels along its edges.

Rule:
- The anchor stays in place; remaining sprites attach one at a time.
- Each sprite is translated so that one of its red pixels overlaps an unpaired red pixel on the growing assembly.
- Every red connector must pair with exactly one other, forming a complete joined structure.

Key insight: Red pixels act as jigsaw pegs — each must match exactly one counterpart, and the assembly order is determined by which unpaired connectors remain.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="a251c730" lines="8-21">
<description>
Stamp source sprites into a destination frame via color-matched singleton markers.

Input structure:
- Two bordered rectangles: source (multi-cell sprites) and destination (singleton markers).
- Each sprite has an anchor pixel — its least-frequent color. Markers share these colors.

Rule:
- Match each destination marker to the source sprite with the same anchor color.
- Translate the sprite so its anchor overlaps the marker position.
- Output is the destination rectangle, interior cleared before stamping.

Key insight: The rarest color per sprite doubles as a template key and positioning anchor.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="c4d067a0" lines="9-23">
<description>
Complete a grid of colored blocks to match a minimap legend in the top-left corner.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Top-left corner: a small sprite of single colored pixels forming a minimap pattern on a regular lattice.
- Main area: uniformly colored square blocks (NxN) arranged on a regular grid; some positions are empty (bg).

Rule:
- The legend pixels define a color pattern; each pixel's lattice position maps to a block position in the main grid via a fixed scale factor and offset.
- Existing colored blocks confirm the scale/offset alignment.
- For every legend pixel whose corresponding grid position is empty, place a new block of that color.

Key insight: The top-left pixel pattern is a scaled-down minimap of the block layout; the mapping is inferred from blocks already present.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="581f7754" lines="8-22">
<description>
Slide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color "marker" pixel.
- Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.

Rule:
- Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.
- Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.
- Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row = anchor row.

Key insight: The anchor's edge position determines the slide axis — top/bottom means horizontal, left/right (or corner) means vertical.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="a6f40cea" lines="74-88">
<description>
Complete partial paths inside a framed rectangle by projecting external color traces inward.

Input structure:
- bg, a 1px rectangular frame (border color) with uniform interior fill.
- Colored components outside the frame touching its edges.

Rule:
- Output = the frame's interior, filled with the fill color.
- Map each outside component's frame-touch points into interior coordinates.
- Monochrome components become solid Manhattan stripes between mapped endpoints.
- Two-color components become alternating-color Manhattan paths.

Key insight: Endpoint colors of two-color traces anchor the alternating phase deterministically.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="dfadab01" lines="79-93">
<description>
Expand each colored control pixel into its corresponding fixed 4x4 glyph.

Input structure:
- Black background with single-pixel markers in colors 2, 3, 5, and/or 8.
- Some inputs also show a sample 4x4 glyph in the output color, with a same-rule marker just below-right of the sample.

Rule:
- Replace each marker color by its learned 4x4 glyph: 2->yellow frame, 3->blue ring, 5->pink diagonal blocks, 8->orange X.
- Paint each glyph with its top-left corner at the marker pixel, clipping at the grid boundary.
- Remove the sample glyphs and skip their below-right label markers.

Key insight:
- The colored pixels are control pixels for a fixed glyph alphabet; non-control sample glyphs are only legend entries, not preserved scene objects.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="6e4f6532" lines="26-41">
<description>
Dock train sprites onto station groups by matching coupler count, then orient via divider-color markers.

Input structure:
- bg; colored stripes (edge + internal) partition grid into rectangular regions.
- Color 8 forms train bodies; color 9 marks both isolated stations and train-attached couplers.
- Stripe-colored pixels on trains are orientation markers.

Rule:
- Stations = 8-connected 9-clusters not adjacent to any 8. Trains = components containing 8.
- Match train to station group where |couplers| == |station size|.
- Rotate/flip train so marker pixels face the same-color boundary stripe of the target region.
- Translate so couplers align exactly onto station cells; erase original position.

Key insight: Coupler count selects the docking target; marker color encodes which boundary stripe to face, fixing the unique orientation.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="eval" puzzle_id="bf45cf4b" lines="7-19">
<description>
Scale up a monochrome control sprite by stamping a polychrome tile at each filled pixel.

Input structure:
- bg = background color.
- Two foreground objects (8-connected): a monochrome rectangular sprite with bg holes (control), and a polychrome square tile (block).

Rule:
- For each cell in the control sprite's bbox: if filled, stamp a copy of the block tile; if bg, leave a tile-sized bg region.
- Output grid dimensions are (control_h × tile_h) by (control_w × tile_w).

Key insight: The control sprite acts as a binary mask that gets "pixel-scaled" by the tile — each pixel expands to a full tile copy or empty space.
</description>

